# Project structure (WebApp)

Keep code in the folder that matches its role. Root: `src/WebApp/`.

| Folder | Purpose |
|--------|---------|
| **Endpoints/** | MVC controllers named `*Endpoints.cs` (e.g. `AuthEndpoints`). Register via `AddControllers` / `MapControllers` in `Startup.cs`. |
| **Components/** | Blazor UI (pages, layout, shared). See `blazor-conventions.mdc`. |
| **Data/** | Blazor-facing backend: `Features`, `Persistence`, `Database.cs` (`AppDbContext`). |
| **AI/** | App AI behavior: MAF agents, workflows, tools, memory, and orchestration (C#). `_Legacy_Python/` is reference only. |
| **Models/** | DTOs, entities, constants. |
| **Utilities/** | Cross-cutting code: `Extensions/`, `Services/`, `Helpers/`. See Utilities section below. |

## Endpoints

- One file per area: `AuthEndpoints.cs`, `ChatEndpoints.cs`, etc. (class name matches file name, not `*Controller`)
- Namespace: `WebApp.Endpoints`
- Each class inherits `Controller` and uses `[Route("api/...")]`, `[HttpPost]`, `[HttpGet]`, etc.
- Wire routing in `Startup.cs` with `MapControllers()` (no separate extension file)
- Blazor pages use UI routes (e.g. `/auth/login`); HTTP actions live under `/api/...` (e.g. `/api/auth/login`)

Do not put HTTP routes under `Components/` or `Data/`.

## Components

- All Blazor UI under `Components/` — `Pages/` (routable; nested `Pages/{Area}/` when routes share a prefix like `auth/`), `Layout/` (layout roots), `Shared/` (non-routable), plus `BaseComponent.cs` at the folder root.
- Follow `blazor-conventions.mdc` for directive order, `@code` regions, and naming.
- Scoped styles: `Component.razor.css` beside the `.razor` file.

## Utilities

Group code by **concern** into one file per concern per subfolder: `{Concern}{FolderKind}.cs` (e.g. `AuthServices.cs`, `AuthExtensions.cs`).

| Subfolder | Purpose | File vs types |
|-----------|---------|----------------|
| **Extensions/** | Static extension methods | One `{Concern}Extensions` class per file; **class name matches file** (e.g. `AuthExtensions.cs` → `AuthExtensions`). |
| **Helpers/** | Static utilities (no DI) | One `{Concern}Helpers` class per file; **class name matches file** (e.g. `CryptographyHelpers.cs` → `CryptographyHelpers`). |
| **Services/** | DI-registered types (`AddScoped`, etc.) | File name is the concern bucket (`AuthServices.cs`). **Types inside use clear names** — they do not have to match the file. One file may hold several related service types (e.g. `CurrentUser`, and later `AuthService` if needed), not only a class called `AuthServices`. |

Example: auth UI reads claims via `CurrentUser` in `Utilities/Services/AuthServices.cs`; sign-in/out stays in **Endpoints** and **Data**.

- Namespaces: `WebApp.Utilities.Extensions`, `WebApp.Utilities.Services`, `WebApp.Utilities.Helpers`
- Do not put EF `DbContext`, HTTP endpoints, or Foundry/AI plumbing under Utilities — use **Data/**, **Endpoints/**, and **AI/Foundry/**

## AI boundaries

- **Agents/**: LLM runners only. One class per agent role at the folder root (no `Chat/` subfolder), e.g. `AssistantAgent`, `EmailAgent`. Agents receive history from orchestration, call `FoundryAgentFactory.Create*Agent()`, and return the turn result. No prompts or deployment names in agent files.
- **Tools/**: Plain C# capabilities exposed to agents via MAF tools. One file per agent domain when needed (e.g. `EmailTools.cs`). Tools may call app services/DB/APIs, but must not call LLMs or route chat turns.
- **Memory/**: Agent-specific memory helpers (summaries, mailbox context, pinned facts), parallel to **Tools/** — one file per agent domain when needed. No LLM calls. Keep `.gitkeep` until the first file is added.
- **Workflows/**: Multi-step MAF workflows that compose agents and tools. Keep placeholder `.gitkeep` files in empty workflow folders.
- **Orchestration/**: Turn-level coordination: `ChatOrchestrator` loads recent thread messages from persistence, routes by `ChatAgent`, and returns the result. No LLM calls in orchestration.
- **Foundry/**: Azure OpenAI / MAF plumbing — `FoundryAgentDefinitions.cs` (deployment names + instructions per agent), `FoundryServices.cs` (`FoundryAgentFactory` with `CreateAssistantAgent` / `CreateEmailAgent` and private endpoint helpers). Do not put prompts in `appsettings`. Register AI types in `Startup.InjectAi`.
- **Models/DTOs.cs**: Keep small AI/config request/result/intent DTOs in the `# AI` region instead of an `AI/Contracts` folder.
- Do not add a `Skills/` folder unless a future capability layer has a clear responsibility distinct from agents and workflows.

## Foundry auth

- Use key-based Azure OpenAI configuration for now: `Foundry:Enabled`, `Foundry:Endpoint`, and `Foundry:ApiKey`.
- Put keys in user secrets or host environment variables (never commit keys).
